  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improving Occupational Health &amp; Safety at work in Andean mines</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The demand of metals has increased with the global energy transition. By the end of 2021 in Peru for instance, employment generated by mining reported more than 227,000 workers, an increase of 30.8% compared to 2020. Increased demand may increase labour violations and Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) risks in a value chain, as companies are not always prepared to cope with these demands. The root causes of the incidents and accidents are not clear, but many are related to different labour rights risks (e.g. lack of adequate protection equipment, long working hours, lack of training on machinery and OSH, flexible contracts, etc). As the metals value chain is complex by nature (due to the smelting processes within the chain), transparency is essential to address any risks related to the metals value chain.This proposal aims to address labour rights violations and Occupational Safety and Health (OSH) risks in the metal supply chain in Bolivia and Peru, among the largest producers of copper, zinc, silver, tin, molybdenum and lead globally. It is a joint project between CNV Internationaal, its local trade union partners, Tata Steel IJmuiden and Wuppermann Staal Nederland, partners of the IRBC Metals Agreement and local production companies within their value chains.Project A starts with a stakeholder mapping and is followed by an assessment of the root causes of OSH risks in the value chain and the lack of transparency. Also a due diligence research of the Dutch companies will be performed. Result of project A is a baseline on accidents and incidents rates per mine site, region, country.Subsequently, in project B the plan is to increase transparency and implement actions to reduce OSH risks in the value chain.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This project aims to reduce OSH risks and accidents for mineworkers in Peru and Bolivia and will serve as a methodology on OSH in the metals industry to be applied in other countries.</narrative>
  </description>
